              <tr> 
                <td width="97%"> 
                  <div align="center"><font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="+2" color="#CC0000">
                    Crucial 512MB PC2100 <b>DDR</b>-SDRAM</font></div>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table></td>
        </tr>
        <tr> 
          <td bgcolor="#FFFFFF"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
              <tr> 
                <td> 
                  <div align="center">
                    <img src="http://www.breneware.com/images/xpDDR.gif" width="180" height="180"></div>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-1">DDR 
              Ram isn't as new a technology as you may think, Graphics cards have 
              been using these chips for well over a year now - to great success. 
              DDR simply means, "Double Data Rate" - theoretically twice the transfer 
              rate of Normal SDRAM (Only Single Data Rate). There are 2 flavors 
              of DDR Ram, PC-1600, and PC-2100. Both are DDR Versions of the PC-100 
              and PC-133 Ram, hence PC-1600 = DDR PC-100. All AMD Athlon based 
              CPU's can use this Ram at PC-1600 spec, but only the very latest 
              133 Mhz bus Athlon CPU's can use PC-2100.</font></p>
            <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-1">Experience 
              the next leap in PC performance. With memory bandwidth being one 
              of the biggest bottlenecks in today's computers, DDR SDRAM breaks 
              through the memory bandwidth barrier by doubling data transfer rate 
              of SDRAM from 1,064 Mb/s to 2,128Mb/s! DDR SDRAM memory will allow 
              your DDR capable motherboard to deliver the worlds fastest gaming 
              experience and new levels of productivity for creative professionals.</font></p>
            <hr>
            <font size="-1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><b>What's 
            DDR-SDRAM?</b></font> 
            <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-1">Although 
              the technical realization may be a lot more difficult, the basic 
              principle of DDR-SDRAM is very simple. While the new memory module 
              is clocked at the same speed as normal SDRAM, it is able to transport 
              double the amount of data by using the rising as well as falling 
              edge of the clock signal for data transfers. We are all aware of 
              this technology since AGP2x and JEDEC is already working on the 
              DDR II spec, which will double the data transfer once more, using 
              the quad-pumped technology known from AGP4x or the upcoming Pentium 
              4 bus. DDR-SDRAM has another important improvement over PC133 SDRAM. 
              Its voltage supply is using only 2.5 V, instead of 3.3 V. This and 
              the lower capacities inside the memory chips lead to a significantly 
              reduced power consumption, which makes DDR-SDRAM also very attractive 
              for notebooks. </font></p>
            
          </td>
